in this video we'll study about the  spinothalamic pathways the spinothalamic  pathway is a group of nerve fibers that  runs in the anterior and lateral part of  the spinal cord and carries the  information like pain temperature  pressure and crew touch to the brain  this is the cross section of the spinal  cord showing the position of the  spinothalamic pathway it is made up of  two distinct nerve fiber tracts anterior  spinothalamic tract and the lateral  spinothalamic tract the anterior spinal  thermic tract carries the sensation of  crude dodging pressure whereas a lateral  spinothalamic tract carries the  sensation of pain and temperature before  progressing further it is important to  understand the concept of lamina of  spinal cord also known as the rx-8  laminate this is a section of central  butterfly-shaped gray matter of the  spinal cord which is divided into ten  lamina named one to ten from posterior  to anterior for the spinothalamic tract  we are mainly concerned with the first  for laminar lamina to is known as the  substantial gelatin OSA and lamina three  and four collectively are known as the  nucleus pro prius now this is a dorsal  nerve root of the spinal cord and the  neurons presented are the pseudo  unipolar neurons these are the  first-order neurons of the spinothalamic  tract the peripheral process of these  neurons carries the information from all  these receptors and the central process  of these neurons relays this information  into this panel cord now the central  process which brings information from  pain and temperature receptors forms a  lateral spinothalamic tract and these  nerve fibers first relay in the second  lamina of the spinal cord whereas the  nerve fibers which break information  from the crew touch and pressure form  the anterior spinal thalmic tract and  these relay into the third and fourth  lamina of the spinal cord in these  lamina these neurons form synapses with  the second order neurons which  immediately cross to the opposite side  of the spinal cord and travel up to the  thalamus on the opposite side of this  Kord so now we know how the first-order  neurons of the spinothalamic tract relay  into the spinal cord and form synapses  with the second-order neurons let's  further understand how these spinal  thermic tract is organized up till the  brain here you see multiple  cross-sections of spinal cord from  lumbar spinal cord up till mid brain and  a section of cerebrum and thalamus the  spinothalamic tract begins when the  nerve fibers from the upper and lower  part of the body enter the spinal cord  at respective levels the nerve fibers  from the lower part of the body enter at  the lumbar spinal cord form synapses in  the exit lamina of the spinal cord from  here second order neurons arise which  cross to the opposite side and form the  drug which travels up to the thalamus on  the opposite side of spinal cord in a  similar way the nerve fibers from the  upper part of the body enter a dist  cervical spinal cord cross to the  opposite side and travel to the thalamus  in the thalamus the second order neurons  form synapses with the third order  neurons these neurons project to the  primary sensory cortex of the brain this  area is also known as the post central  gyrus where all these sensations are  perceived so this is a brief overview  about the spinothalamic pathway thank  you so much for watching this video like  our channel on YouTube for all the  upcoming videos and make sure to LIKE  our channel on Facebook for flashcards  and upcoming notifications
 

